768767"},"200220084":{"uid":"200220084","accession":"GSE220084","gds":"","title":"Role of immediate early genes in the development of salivary gland organoids in polyisocyanopeptide hydrogels","summary":"Human salivary gland organoids have opened tremendous possibilities for regenerative medicine in patients undergoing radiotherapy for the treatment of head and neck cancer. However, their clinical translation is greatly limited by the current use of Matrigel for organoid derivation and expansion. Here, we envisage that the use of a fully, synthetic hydrogel based on the oligo(-ethylene glycol) functionalized polymer polyisocyanopeptides (PICs) can provide an environment suitable for the generation and expansion of salivary gland organoids (SGOs) after optimization of PIC polymer properties. We demonstrate that PIC hydrogels decorated with the cell-binding peptide RGD allow SGO formation from salivary gland (SG)-derived stem cells. This self-renewal potential is preserved for only 4 passages. It was found that SGOs differentiated prematurely in PIC hydrogels affecting their self-renewal capacity. Similarly, SGOs show decreased expression of immediate early genes (IEGs) after culture in PIC hydrogels. Activation of multiple signalling pathways involved in IEG expression by β-adrenergic agonist isoproterenol, led to increased stem cell self-renewal capacity as measured by organoid forming efficiency (OFE). These results indicate that PIC hydrogels are promising 3D matrices for SGOs, with the option to be used clinically, after further optimization of the hydrogel and culture conditions.","gpl":"18573","gse":"220084","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/12/10","suppfile":"CSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6787181","title":"Tissue_024"},{"accession":"GSM6787201","title":"PIC_14d_060"},{"accession":"GSM6787193","title":"Matrigel_14d_049_2"},{"accession":"GSM6787187","title":"Matrigel_14d_013"},{"accession":"GSM6787183","title":"Matrigel_7d_012"},{"accession":"GSM6787186","title":"Matrigel_7d_013"},{"accession":"GSM6787189","title":"Matrigel_14d_045"},{"accession":"GSM6787192","title":"Matrigel_14d_049"},{"accession":"GSM6787195","title":"Matrigel_14d_060"},{"accession":"GSM6787191","title":"Matrigel_7d_049"},{"accession":"GSM6787194","title":"Matrigel_7d_060"},{"accession":"GSM6787200","title":"PIC_14d_049"},{"accession":"GSM6787180","title":"Tissue_021"},{"accession":"GSM6787197","title":"PIC_14d_013"},{"accession":"GSM6787182","title":"Tissue_025"},{"accession":"GSM6787185","title":"Matrigel_14d_012"},{"accession":"GSM6787188","title":"Matrigel_7d_045"},{"accession":"GSM6787199","title":"PIC_14d_045"},{"accession":"GSM6787196","title":"PIC_14d_012"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":19,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36818041"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE220nnn/GSE220084/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA908784"},"200182227":{"uid":"200182227","accession":"GSE182227","gds":"","title":"Cellular states are coupled to genomic and viral heterogeneity in HPV-related oropharyngeal carcinoma","summary":"Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) includes a large subset of cancers that are driven by the human papilloma virus (HPV) and occur primarily in the oropharynx. Here, we use 10x single cell RNA-seq to profile 70,970 cells from 11 HPV-positive and 5 HPV-negative oropharyngeal tumors in order to uncover diversity in chromosomal aberrations, cellular states and viral gene expression between and within tumors.","gpl":"24676","gse":"182227","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/11/30","suppfile":"MTX, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5525410","title":"OP35-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM5525393","title":"OP6-CD45-"},{"accession":"GSM5525390","title":"OP4-CD45-"},{"accession":"GSM5525407","title":"OP33-normal"},{"accession":"GSM5525404","title":"OP19"},{"accession":"GSM5525401","title":"OP14-CD45-"},{"accession":"GSM5525408","title":"OP34-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM5525405","title":"OP20"},{"accession":"GSM5525402","title":"OP16"},{"accession":"GSM5525403","title":"OP17"},{"accession":"GSM5525399","title":"OP13"},{"accession":"GSM5525400","title":"OP14-CD45+"},{"accession":"GSM5525397","title":"OP10"},{"accession":"GSM5525396","title":"OP9-CD45-"},{"accession":"GSM5525394","title":"OP8"},{"accession":"GSM5525411","title":"OP35-LN"},{"accession":"GSM5525391","title":"OP5"},{"accession":"GSM5525395","title":"OP9-CD45+"},{"accession":"GSM5525392","title":"OP6-CD45+"},{"accession":"GSM5525398","title":"OP12"},{"accession":"GSM5525389","title":"OP4-CD45+"},{"accession":"GSM5525406","title":"OP33-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM5525412","title":"OP35-normal"},{"accession":"GSM5525409","title":"OP34-normal"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP332742","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP332/SRP332742/"}],"n_samples":24,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["37012457"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE182nnn/GSE182227/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA755331"},"200210287":{"uid":"200210287","accession":"GSE210287","gds":"","title":"Towards a mechanistic understanding of patient response to neoadjuvant SBRT with anti-PDL1 in human HPV-unrelated locally advanced HNSCC: Phase I/Ib trial results","summary":"Five-year survival for HPV-unrelated head and neck squamous cell carcinomas (HNSCC) remains below 50%. We assessed the safety of administering combination hypofractionated stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) with anti-PDL-1 neoadjuvantly followed by adjuvant anti-PDL-1 with standard of care therapy (n=21) (NCT03635164). The primary endpoint of the study was safety, which was met. Secondary endpoints included radiographic, pathologic, and objective response, locoregional control (LRC), progression-free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS). Among evaluable patients at early median follow-up of 16 months (448 days, or 64 weeks), OS was 80.1% with 95% C.I. (62.0%, 100.0%), LRC and PFS were 75.8% with 95% C.I. (57.5%, 99.8%), and major pathological response (MPR) or complete response (CR) was 75% with 95% exact C.I. (51.6%, 100%). Circulating CD8/Treg ratio, CD4 effector memory T cells, and TCR repertoire at time of surgery emerged as biologic correlates of response to therapy. Using high-dimensional multi-omics and spatial data as well as biological correlatives pre- and post-treatment, three major changes were noted in responders within the tumor microenvironment (TME) (and within the blood) post-treatment: 1) an increase in effector T cells; 2) a decrease in immunosuppressive cells; and 3) an increase in antigen presentation. Non-responders appeared to fail due to a lack of one of these three identified steps needed for priming and maintaining activation of T cells. Multiple correlates for response, along with subsets of non-responders that may benefit from additional or alternative immunotherapies, were identified. This treatment is being tested in an ongoing phase II trial with a similar design, where we hope to confirm and expand on our understanding of the mechanisms underlying resistance to therapy.","gpl":"18573","gse":"210287","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/11/02","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6427644","title":"Post, R [S15]"},{"accession":"GSM6427647","title":"Post, R [S19]"},{"accession":"GSM6427650","title":"Pre, NR [S22]"},{"accession":"GSM6427635","title":"Pre, N