 pro-inflammatory cytokine IL-1beta. Models of early-stage HPV infection (human keratinocytes expressing HPV16 E6 and E7) and models of established HPV-positive head and neck cancers (patient-derived xenografts, head and neck cancer cell lines) exhibited similar dysregulation of IL-1 pathway genes and suppressed responses to IL-1beta treatment. Such overlap in cell responses supports that changes induced by HPV E6 and E7 early in infection could persist and contribute to a dysregulated immune environment throughout carcinogenesis. HPV E6 and E7 also drove the upregulation of several suppressors of IL-1 cytokine signaling, including SIGIRR, both in primary keratinocytes and in cancer cells. SIGIRR knockout was insufficient to increase IL-1beta-dependent gene expression in the presence of HPV16 E6 and E7, suggesting that multiple suppressors of IL-1 signaling contribute to dampened IL-1 responses in HPV16-positive cells.","gpl":"24676","gse":"211730","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/10/31","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6500318","title":"16E6-E7-A-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500315","title":"16E6-E7-A-untreated"},{"accession":"GSM6500319","title":"16E6-E7-B-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500313","title":"GFP-GFP-B-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500310","title":"GFP-GFP-B-untreated"},{"accession":"GSM6500316","title":"16E6-E7-B-untreated"},{"accession":"GSM6500311","title":"GFP-GFP-C-untreated"},{"accession":"GSM6500317","title":"16E6-E7-C-untreated"},{"accession":"GSM6500314","title":"GFP-GFP-C-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500312","title":"GFP-GFP-A-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500320","title":"16E6-E7-C-IL1B"},{"accession":"GSM6500309","title":"GFP-GFP-A-untreated"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36342298"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE211nnn/GSE211730/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA871905"},"200182459":{"uid":"200182459","accession":"GSE182459","gds":"","title":"macroH2A2 overexpression in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma cell line T-HEp3.","summary":"We report the RNA expression profile in the restoration of macroH2A2 in tumorigenic (T-HEp3) HNSCC cell line. T-HEp3 is a human epidermoid carcinoma, which grows on chorioallantoic membreane and metastasizes with high efficency into the organs of chick embryo. Upon serial paasage for more than 50 times in vitro , T-HEp3 loses tumorigenic and metastatic potential and termed as dormant D-HEp3. This is an ideal cell line model system to study cancer dormancy.","gpl":"18573","gse":"182459","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/10/31","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5529737","title":"mh2a2_with_serum_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5529735","title":"mh2a2_with_serum_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5529732","title":"h2b_with_serum_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5529733","title":"h2b_with_serum_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5529736","title":"mh2a2_with_serum_rep2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP333362","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP333/SRP333362/"}],"n_samples":5,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36459552"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE182nnn/GSE182459/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA756293"},"200200421":{"uid":"200200421","accession":"GSE200421","gds":"","title":"A Transcriptomic Map of EGFR-induced Epithelial-to-Mesenchymal Transition Identifies Prognostic and Therapeutic Targets for Head and Neck Cancer","summary":"Purpose: We aim to identify transcriptomic changes associated with EGFR-mediated epithelial to mesenchymal transition in carcinoma cells of the upper aerodigestive tract Method: Kyse30 and FaDu (ATCC, Manassas, VA, USA) were passaged in DMEM or RPMI, 10% FCS, 1% penicillin/streptomycin, 5% CO2 atmosphere at 37°C. Treatment with EGF (PromoCell PromoKine, Heidelberg, Germany), EpEX-Fc (Pan et al. PMID: 30261040) were conducted under serum-free conditions for 6h and 72h. Results:  Kyse30 cells: At 6h/72h of treatment high-dose EGF (9nM) induced 612/1208 genes; low-dose EGF (1.8nM) induced 397/0 genes; EpEX-Fc (50nM) induced 137/2 genes; high-dose EGF (9nM) + EpEX (50nM) induced 291/0 genes  FaDu cells: At 6h/72h of treatment high-dose EGF (9nM) induced 994/1536 genes; low-dose EGF (1.8nM) induced 671/4 genes; EpEX-Fc (50nM) induced 36/0 genes; high-dose EGF (9nM) + EpEX (50nM) induced 911/103 genes Conclusion: High- and low-dose EGF treatment in Kyse30 and FaDu cells differ with respect to short-term and long-term gene regulation. High-dose EGF, which is associated with induction of EMT, induces a higher number of differentially regulated genes (DEGs) after 6h and has a sustained strong regulatory capacity at 72h, when other treatmernts showed no DEGs.","gpl":"20301","gse":"200421","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/10/26","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6033062","title":"Fadu_control 6h_2"},{"accession":"GSM6033042","title":"Kyse_control 6h_4"},{"accession":"GSM6033045","title":"Kyse_EGF 1_8nM 6h_4"},{"accession":"GSM6033082","title":"Fadu_control 6h_4"},{"accession":"GSM6033085","title":"Fadu_EGF 1_8nM 6h_4"},{"accession":"GSM6033039","title":"Kyse_EGF 1_8nM 72h_3"},{"accession":"GSM6033065","title":"Fadu_EGF 9nM 6h_2"},{"accession":"GSM6033019","title":"Kyse_EpEX 72h_1"},{"accession":"GSM6033059","title":"Fadu_EpEX 72h_1"},{"accession":"GSM6033022","title":"Kyse_EGF 1_8nM 6h_2"},{"accession":"GSM603307