GSE214146/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA884118"},"200214145":{"uid":"200214145","accession":"GSE214145","gds":"","title":"Gene expression profile of patient-derived head and neck squamous carcinoma cells subjected to different treatments.","summary":"In order to investigate the mechanisms underlying response and resistance towards treatment in HNSCC, we used low passage in vitro HNSCC cultures from two patients, namely SJG26 and SJG17. Both cultures were subjected to selection by AG1478-mediated EGFR inhibition, radiation, as well as the combination of both for two weeks, generating presumably tolerant cell populations (AG, R and RAG). These tolerant cell populations and a non-treated population were subjected to bulk RNA-seq to study the overall gene expression profile of the cell populations.","gpl":"18573","gse":"214145","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/09/28","suppfile":"TAB","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6600098","title":"SJG26_NT_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600104","title":"SJG26_RAG_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600101","title":"SJG26_R_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600112","title":"SJG17_RAG_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600099","title":"SJG26_AG_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600107","title":"SJG17_AG_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600110","title":"SJG17_R_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600111","title":"SJG17_RAG_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600108","title":"SJG17_AG_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600105","title":"SJG17_NT_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600102","title":"SJG26_R_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600106","title":"SJG17_NT_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6600109","title":"SJG17_R_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600103","title":"SJG26_RAG_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600097","title":"SJG26_NT_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6600100","title":"SJG26_AG_rep2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":16,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE214nnn/GSE214145/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA884116"},"200179223":{"uid":"200179223","accession":"GSE179223","gds":"","title":"RNA-seq analysis evaluating cell cycle specific gene expression and the effect of KRTAP2-3 gene inactivation in SAS cells expressing Fucci.","summary":"Proliferation and migration of cancer cells are regulated by the stimuli from tumour microenvironment during its progression and metastasis. While transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β) increases migration and metastasis of various types of epithelial cancer cells via induction of epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT), it also decreases their proliferation by inducing G1 cell cycle arrest. However, the correlation of these tumour promoting and suppressing effects of TGF-β remains to be elusive because of the lack of analysis at a single cell level. Here, we showed that TGF-β confers the oral cancer cells residing in G1 phase with higher motility and mesenchymal phenotypes, suggesting a correlation between TGF-β-dependent cell cycle arrest and increased migration. We identified keratin-associated protein 2-3 (KRTAP2-3) as a regulator of these dual effects of TGF-β. Expression of KRTAP2-3 was associated with both TGF-β-induced cell cycle arrest and increased migration, and also was correlated with poor prognosis of head and neck cancer patients. We found that deletion of KRTAP2-3 gene decreased in vitro migration and in vivo metastasis of oral cancer cells via induction of mesenchymal-epithelial transition (MET). KRTAP2-3 also induces the expression of Regulator of G-protein signalling 4 (RGS4) and Zinc finger BED domain-containing protein 2 (ZBED2), which suppresses proliferation and increases migration of oral cancer cells, respectively. Present findings revealed novel mechanisms how TGF-β orchestrates proliferation and migration of cancer cells by inducing the expression of KRTAP2-3, and highlighted the importance of targeting the motile cancer cells under G1 cell cycle arrest to suppress metastasis.","gpl":"17303","gse":"179223","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/09/27","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5411675","title":"A G1 phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, TGF-b treated, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM5411678","title":"A clone of SAS cells (control sgRNA), replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411681","title":"A clone of SAS cells (KRTAP2-3 sgRNA), replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM5411679","title":"A clone of SAS cells (KRTAP2-3 sgRNA), replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411670","title":"A G1 phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, unstimulated, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411676","title":"A S/G2/M phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, TGF-b treated, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411677","title":"A S/G2/M phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, TGF-b treated, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM5411671","title":"A G1 phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, unstimulated, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM5411674","title":"A G1 phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, TGF-b treated, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411673","title":"A S/G2/M phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, unstimulated, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM5411672","title":"A S/G2/M phase fraction of SAS-Fucci cells, unstimulated, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM5411680","title":"A clone of SAS cells (control sgRNA), replicate 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP326341","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP326/SRP326341/"}],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36170816"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE179nnn/GSE179223/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA742755"},"200186082":{"uid":"200186082","accession":"GSE186082","gds":"","title":"Circulating tumor cell-derived N-cadherin induces NK cell exhaustion and contributes to tumor self-seeding via the KLRG1 receptor","summary":"Circulating tumor cells (CTCs) can survive in the circulation and return to primary tumors through a self-seeding process. However, the mechanisms underlying CTC escape from natural killer (NK) cell-mediated immune surveillance remain unclear. In the present study, self-seeded tumor cells were isolated and characterized by applying a modified mouse model. The harvested self-seeded cells displayed resistance to NK cell-mediated lysis and a higher tumor seeding ability than their parental cells. Elevated expression levels of the CDH2 gene and its protein product, N-cadherin were found in self-seeded cell lines. We revealed an unrecognized role for soluble N-cadherin in inhibiting NK cell antitumor immunity. NK cells secreted cytokines, and fluid shear stress facilitated N-cadherin release from tumor cells by enhancing the activity of A disintegrin and metalloprotease 10 (ADAM10). Soluble N-cadherin triggered NK cell functional exhaustion by interacting with the killer cell lectin-like receptor subfamily G member 1 (KLRG1) receptor and therefore protected tumor cells from NK cell killing. Elevated N-cadherin expression was observed in recurrent oral cancer samples compared to matched primary tumors. Our findings illustrated an unknown mechanism by which CTCs evaded NK cell-mediated immune surveillance, which may contribute to tumor self-seeding and recurrence.","gpl":"20301","gse":"186082","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/09/21","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5631627","title":"SCC9-parental"},{"accession":"GSM5631626","title":"SCC9-seeded"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP341990","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP341/SRP341990/"}],"n_samples":2,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36096526"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE186nnn/GSE186082/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA772307"},"200184567":{"uid":"200184567","accession":"GSE184567","gds":"","title":"TRIM67 drives tumorigenesis in oligodendrogliomas through Rho GTPase-dependent membrane blebbing","summary":"The aim of this study is to characterize the mechanistic role of TRIM67 in glioma biogenesis. Towards this goal, here we utilized engineered human neural stem cells and patient-derived tumor sphere lines with or without IDH mutations and 1p/19q co-deletions to study the function of TRIM67 in gliomas. We found that upregulated TRIM67 induces membrane blebbing through Rho GTPase-mediated signaling, resulting in more advanced cell motility and tumor expansion.","gpl":"20301","gse":"184567","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/09/21","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5592674","title":"hNSC_CTRL"},{"accession":"GSM5592683","title":"TS543_TRIM67"},{"accession":"GSM5592677","title":"TS603_TRIM67"},{"accession":"GSM5592680","title":"TS600_CTRL"},{"accession":"GSM5592676","title":"TS603_CTRL"},{"accession":"GSM5592679","title":"S24_TRIM67"},{"accession":"GSM5592682","title":"TS543_CTRL"},{"accession":"GSM5592681","title":"TS600_TRIM67"},{"accession":"GSM5592675","title":"hNSC_TRIM67"},{"accession":"GSM5592678","title":"S24_CTRL"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP338116","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP338/SRP338116/"}],"n_samples":10,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36215168"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE184nnn/GSE184567/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA765042"},"200213047":{"uid":"200213047","accession":"GSE213047","gds":"","title":"Single-cell transcriptomic profiling for inferring tumor origin and mechanisms of therapeutic resistance  ","summary":"We performed single-cell RNA sequencing on three different tumors isolated from an HNSCC patient who had a mixed response to checkpoint inhibitor therapy. A total of 11,470 single cells were profiled after the removal of cells that did not pass QC: Tumor-1: NPSCC (PR; n=3818; HN19-1); Tumor-2: NPSCC lymph node (PR; n=2870; HN19-2); and Tumor-3: Laryngeal SCC (PD; n=4782; HN19-3).","gpl":"18573","gse":"213047","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/09/14","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6569752","title":"Tumor-2, NPSCC lymph node (PR)"},{"accession":"GSM6569753","title":"Tumor-3, Laryngeal SCC (PD)"},{"accession":"GSM6569751","title":"Tumor-1, NPSCC (PR)"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":3,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["36210388"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE213nnn/GSE213047/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA878830"}}}
